{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.066,
            0.08,
            0.315,
            0.218
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.072,
                0.226,
                0.314,
                0.274
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.5: Friction acts between two surfaces and opposes the motion of the object."
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_0.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.325,
            0.078,
            0.883,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": "On pushing, the object stops after sliding a certain distance. This must be due to a force acting between the surfaces of the sliding object and the table or floor which are in contact. This force must be acting on the object in a direction opposite to its direction of motion. This force is what brings the object to a stop."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.326,
            0.191,
            0.883,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": "The force that comes into play when an object moves or tries to move over another surface is called the **force of friction** or simply **friction**. Friction always acts in a direction opposite to the direction in which the object is moving or trying to move. The force of friction is a contact"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.283,
            0.748,
            0.301
          ],
          "content": "force since it arises due to two surfaces in contact."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.304,
            0.883,
            0.398
          ],
          "content": "Friction arises due to the irregularities in the two surfaces in contact. Even surfaces which appear smooth, have a large number of minute irregularities (Fig. 5.6). When placed in contact, the irregularities of two surfaces lock into each other and oppose any effort to move one surface over the other."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.06,
            0.381,
            0.147,
            0.495
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.19,
            0.418,
            0.376,
            0.489
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Does this mean that the force of friction will be greater if the surfaces are rough?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.47,
            0.398,
            0.72,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.398,
                0.486,
                0.866,
                0.504
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.6: Friction between two surfaces due to irregularities"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.521,
            0.622,
            0.545
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 5.4: Let us explore"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.564,
            0.883,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": "z Try Activity 5.3 again, but this time place the same object on different surfaces, such as glass, cloth, wood, ceramic tile, and sand."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.626,
            0.882,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": "z Does the object stop after travelling the same distance as in Activity 5.3?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.668,
            0.851,
            0.687
          ],
          "content": "z Does the object stop at the same distance on all surfaces?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.692,
            0.883,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": "For different surfaces, the object stops after moving different distances so we can say that the force of friction depends upon the nature of the surfaces in contact. Friction is greater on rough surfaces."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.083,
            0.769,
            0.253,
            0.92
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.774,
            0.43,
            0.795
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A step further"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.261,
            0.811,
            0.862,
            0.915
          ],
          "content": "Does the force of friction act only if the objects are moving on solid surfaces? What about objects moving through liquids and gases? Air, water, and other liquids also exert force of friction on the objects moving through them. Hence the objects, such as aeroplanes, ships, boats, and high-speed trains are designed with specific shapes to reduce the force of friction due to the air or water around them."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.07,
            0.924,
            0.093,
            0.938
          ],
          "content": "68"
        },
        {
